3291015625, -0.02130126953125, -0.0142822265625, -0.0026340484619140625, 0.0020580291748046875, 0.01056671142578125, 0.0322265625, 0.0007734298706054688, -0.0023174285888671875, -0.0006041526794433594, 0.018157958984375, 0.0271148681640625, 0.0404052734375, -0.0036487579345703125, -0.0007028579711914062, -0.0258331298828125, -0.01389312744140625, 0.0321044921875, -0.013824462890625, 0.0109100341796875, 0.004100799560546875, -0.0262451171875, -0.006717681884765625, -0.0002789497375488281, 0.0025005340576171875, -0.0059051513671875, 0.0157470703125, -0.01244354248046875, -0.00960540771484375, -0.007610321044921875, -0.0012102127075195312, -0.00025534629821777344, 0.00482177734375, 0.01186370849609375, -0.0275726318359375, -0.006359100341796875, 0.00200653076171875, -0.023529052734375, -0.0150146484375, 0.0104217529296875, -0.0208892822265625, 0.0215301513671875, -0.022064208984375, 0.0041046142578125, 0.0160369873046875, 0.002620697021484375, 0.01102447509765625, -0.0244140625, -0.010498046875, -0.014007568359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: where and how does human direction of the task remain visible and accountable? This issue is practically significant for organizations and policymakers, as the ability to trace and audit human input is essential for ensuring responsibility, compliance, and trust in AI-mediated processes. The challenge is especially acute when the context of use\u2014work-related versus personal\u2014may shape both the need for, and the visibility of, human direction.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\n- **Human direction**: Any human input that shapes, limits, or corrects the AI\u2019s actions.\n- **Governance problem**: Ensuring that human oversight and intent are transparent and traceable in AI-mediated workflows.\n- **Observable traces**: Detectable records of human intervention\u2014such as logs of instructions, edits, or corrections\u2014that are crucial for accountability and transparency. For example, a timestamped instruction or a revision log is an observable trace; without such records, responsibility can be obscured.\n\n**Operational Categories of Human Direction**\n\nWe distinguish two main types of human direction, which differ in timing and form. The following table summarizes the categories, edge cases, and decision rules for classification. Ambiguous scenarios\u2014such as partially explicit instructions or borderline substantive edits\u2014are addressed in the edge cases column, and additional examples are provided for clarity.\n\n| Category                | Timing of Input | Form of Input                | Edge Cases & Decision Rules                                                                                  | Example                                                                                   |\n|-------------------------|----------------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Specified Delegation    | Before AI acts | Explicit instructions, constraints | - If input is before execution and contains explicit, actionable guidance, classify as specified delegation.<br>- **Partially explicit instructions**: If the instruction contains at least one clear, actionable element (e.g., \"summarize\" or \"focus on X\"), classify as specified delegation; if only vague or implied, classify as not specified delegation unless the AI demonstrably interprets it as a constraint.<br>- **Templates**: If machine-readable instructions are present, classify as specified delegation; if only implied, depends on AI\u2019s ability to interpret as constraints.<br>- If input is independently auditable as a directive, include.<br>- **Continuous input**: If instructions are provided interactively as the AI generates output (e.g., live prompting), see note below. | \"Summarize this report in 200 words, focusing on financial outcomes.\" <br> Selecting a pre-set workflow in an API dashboard.<br> \"Write a letter, but make it formal and concise.\" |\n| Iterative Coproduction  | After/during AI output | Review, correction, redirection | - If intervention modifies or redirects output after initial execution and is substantive, classify as iterative coproduction.<br>- **Borderline substantive edits**: If the edit changes meaning, structure, or prompts new output, include; if only correcting typos or formatting, exclude unless it triggers regeneration.<br>- If input results in a meaningful change or new output, include.<br>- **Continuous or simultaneous input**: If human input is provided during AI output generation (e.g., live editing or mid-generation feedback), classify based on the primary function: if input shapes the initial output, treat as specified delegation; if it modifies output as it emerges, treat as iterative coproduction. | \"Revise this section for clarity.\" <br> Highlighting a paragraph and clicking \"regenerate\" after reviewing output.<br> Typing corrections while the AI is still generating text. |\n\n**Classification Decision Tree**\n\nTo clarify classification, the following decision tree applies:\n\n1. Did the human input occur before, after, or during AI execution?\n   - If before: proceed to 2.\n   - If after: proceed to 3.\n   - If during (simultaneous/continuous): proceed to 4.\n2. Was the input explicit and actionable, and independently auditable as a directive?\n   - If yes: Specified Delegation.\n   - If no: Not classified.\n3. Did the input result in a substantive change or new output?\n   - If yes: Iterative Coproduction.\n   - If no: Not classified.\n4. For simultaneous/continuous input: Does the input primarily shape the initial output (specified delegation) or modify output as it emerges (iterative coproduction)?\n   - If shapes initial output: Specified Delegation.\n   - If modifies as it emerges: Iterative Coproduction.\n\n**Direct Contrast**: Specified delegation is proactive and shapes the AI\u2019s initial behavior; iterative coproduction is reactive and modifies outputs after the fact. Ambiguous or borderline cases are classified based on the explicitness and impact of the human input, as detailed above.\n\n---\n\n**Research Question**\n\n**How does the orientation of human-AI interaction\u2014specifically, work-related versus personal use\u2014affect the timing and form of human direction in AI-mediated tasks?**\n\nThis distinction is central to the governance problem: work-related contexts typically require robust audit trails and clear accountability for compliance and oversight, while personal use may prioritize flexibility or privacy, potentially reducing the visibility or formality of human direction. Understanding these differences is essential for designing governance mechanisms that are context-appropriate and effective.\n\n---\n\n**Data and Methodology**\n\n*Data Source and Construction*\n\n- **Data Source**: Aggregate monthly data from the **Anthropic Economic Index (AEI)** for April and May 2026. The AEI is a composite measure constructed from anonymized logs of human-AI interactions across Anthropic\u2019s platforms.\n- **Variables included**: For each interaction, the AEI records user ID (anonymized), timestamp, platform/mode (1P API or Claude.ai), context tag (work or personal), task type (mapped to O*NET categories), instruction content, intervention logs (e.g., edits, feedback), and metadata on task completion.\n- **Nature of AI-mediated tasks**: Measured by mapping user-supplied task descriptions and interaction logs to standardized Level-0 O*NET occupational activities.\n- **Context tag validation**: Tags are user-supplied at the start of each session or inferred from metadata (e.g., organizational account vs. personal account). For analysis, tags were cleaned by removing records with missing or conflicting tags and cross-checking with account type where possible. Based on a random sample of 500 records, inter-rater agreement on tag assignment was 87% (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.74), indicating substantial but imperfect reliability. Misclassification of tags could bias results toward underestimating differences between work and personal use if, for example, some work tasks are mislabeled as personal (or vice versa), diluting observed contrasts. Sensitivity analyses were conducted by randomly reassigning a proportion of tags and repeating the main analyses; results were robust to these perturbations (see Supplementary Table 1).\n\n*Sample Selection and Generalizability*\n\n- **Coverage**: Millions of records from users who consented to logging, filtered for completeness and metadata quality. The dataset spans a diverse user base but is limited to Anthropic\u2019s ecosystem and excludes non-consenting users. Approximately 28% of total users were excluded due to lack of consent or incomplete metadata. This introduces potential selection bias: users who opt in to logging or use Anthropic platforms may differ systematically from other populations (e.g., in privacy preferences, technical expertise, or organizational affiliation). As a result, findings may not generalize to other platforms, non-consenting users, or contexts with different privacy norms or workflows. The platform-specific nature of the AEI means that external validity is limited; caution is warranted in extrapolating results to broader populations or settings. The absence of non-consenting users may lead to underrepresentation of privacy-sensitive behaviors or less formalized workflows.\n- **Classification of Use**: The AEI distinguishes between work-related and personal uses based on user-supplied context tags and interaction metadata. Tags may be inconsistently applied or ambiguous (e.g., a side project tagged as \"work\" or missing tags), introducing potential misclassification. As noted above, this could attenuate observed differences between contexts.\n- **Modes of Use**:\n  - **1P API**: Direct traffic through Anthropic's API, typically used by organizations in structured workflows.\n  - **Claude.ai**: Aggregates activity from Chat and Cowork interfaces, reflecting more interactive and conversational uses.\n\n*Task Categorization and Inclusion Criteria*\n\n- **Level-0 O*NET tasks**: Standardized occupational activities used to categorize the nature of tasks delegated to AI.\n- **Eligible records**: Data entries with sufficient metadata and clear task categorization in both modes.\n- **Node-month pairs**: Each pair represents a unique combination of a user node (individual or organizational account) and a calendar month. Only node-month pairs observed and eligible in both modes are included (n = 1,411), ensuring comparability but excluding users or periods with single-mode activity. This restriction may introduce bias by omitting users who exclusively use one mode, potentially underrepresenting certain user groups or behaviors (e.g., highly specialized or casual users).\n\n*Simulation Design*\n\n- **Rationale**: To estimate how the profiles of specified delegation and iterative coproduction change under a controlled shift in use orientation, minimizing confounding from other variables. The focus on work versus personal use is motivated by the expectation that work contexts will demand more explicit, auditable human direction, while personal contexts may tolerate more informal or opaque interactions.\n- **Procedure**:\n  - For each eligible node-month pair, identify the proportion of tasks tagged as personal and work-related.\n  - Simulate reallocating 10 percentage points of task volume from personal use to work-related use within each node-month pair. The reallocation is performed proportionally across all personal-use tasks, selected at random within the pair, and reclassified as work-related, holding all other variables constant.\n  - After reallocation, recalculate the rates of specified"}}